WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.140
“Okay, okay, just hear me out: what if we
shot it into the sun?”

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.630
Foster asked excitedly.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:10.009
“You are aware just how far away the sun
actually is, right?”

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:14.059
his fellow researcher Rees replied, sighing
with exhaustion at their lengthy conversation;

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:18.131
they had been discussing this for so long
that the food on both their trays had already

00:00:18.131 --> 00:00:20.599
gone unappetizingly cold.

00:00:20.599 --> 00:00:26.010
“Obviously, but think about it: we’d be
sending it into a perpetually-burning nuclear

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.010
furnace.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:31.750
There’s no way it would be able to survive,”
Researcher Foster rebutted, standing by his

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.469
latest hare-brained theory.

00:00:33.469 --> 00:00:37.430
“We send it up in a rocket, fully automated,
so no personnel have to put themselves in

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.300
harm’s way and sacrifice their lives.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.219
Then boom, it flies into the sun.

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:42.219
Problem solved.

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:47.280
“So, it never occurred to you that 682 might
figure out a way to escape on the hundred-and

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.320
fifty-million-kilometer journey?”

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.890
Rees poked at his mashed potatoes with a fork
out of boredom.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:55.620
“You’re forgetting how intelligent the
thing is; it might be an omnicidal killing

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.320
machine, but it’s smart as either of us…

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.850
well, one of us, at least.”

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.430
Sitting opposite each other in the mess hall,
Researcher Foster and Researcher Rees kept

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:10.730
up their bickering, debating on what would
be the best way to neutralize SCP-682, the

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.600
infamous Hard to Destroy Reptile.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.130
It was a discussion nearly every member of
Foundation personnel had been a part of at

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.170
least once: how could they kill this unkillable
beast?

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.210
What other anomalies were they yet to try
and use against it?

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.729
Even after countless tests – most leading
to numerous containment breaches and significant

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:34.140
loss of life – the Foundation seemed to
be no closer to putting a definitive end to

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.140
SCP-682.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:40.870
But while Rees and Foster debated more and
more outlandish possibilities, someone was

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:43.850
sitting nearby, listening in on every word.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:48.950
At a table all by himself in the mess hall,
a man in typical Foundation Security Officer

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:54.170
garb was quietly scrawling everything the
pair of researchers were saying on a notepad.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:58.939
The photo identification and nametag he wore
on his uniform identified him as one Elias

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:04.310
Anderson, a long-serving member of the SCP
Foundation, with an extensive service record

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.719
logged in their personnel database.

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:10.110
Of course, that name and the whole identity
were just a cover.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:15.630
The real Officer Elias Anderson was dead,
his body being kept on ice, hidden in a morgue

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.580
within a secure black site.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.750
It hadn’t taken long for the man now wearing
Anderson’s uniform and using his name to

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.800
‘update’ the officer’s photo with one
of his own face in the Foundation’s database,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.500
keeping his subterfuge so far undetected.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.330
Gathering up his notepad, casually hiding
it under his tray as he stood up and walked

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:38.320
towards the mess hall exit, the unknown infiltrator
made his way to the nearest elevator.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.210
Riding it up to the highest level, making
sure any other passengers had gotten out at

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:46.900
other floors, the ‘Security Officer’ flipped
the emergency stop switch.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.280
He reached into his pocket, retrieving a jury-rigged
handheld communication device, and began sending

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.180
a message on a secure channel.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.150
“Agent Paratos checking in,” he wrote.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.860
“The SCP Foundation is still bickering amongst
themselves about the best way to eliminate

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.080
the target.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.180
SCP-682 remains contained, but alive.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.040
Advise best course of action?”

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.750
The cursor on screen blinked for a few seconds,
awaiting a response.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.400
“Excellent work, Agent,” the reply from
GOC Command finally arrived, lines of text

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.360
scrolling rapidly across the screen.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.630
“As long as this anomaly is allowed to live,
it poses an ever-present threat to all life

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:22.630
on Earth.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.580
What is your assessment of the Foundation’s
approach to termination?”

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.680
Paratos thought to himself for a moment, before
sending back:

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.380
“They’re overthinking it.”

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.290
“Affirmative,” the Coalition wrote.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:37.170
“Have the O5 Council still denied termination
of SCP-682 via nuclear weapons?”

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:42.060
The undercover agent quickly responded, confirming
this was indeed still the case.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.870
There was another pause, almost like the whole
GOC was thinking carefully about what to do

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:47.870
next.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:51.570
“Global Occult Coalition executives have
conferred in private to authorize a Hammer

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:57.800
Down operation to eliminate the anomaly known
as SCP-682,” came the follow-up from Command.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:03.560
“An F-22 Raptor fitted with a sixty megaton
payload will be launched at 0600, bound for

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.140
the following coordinates.”

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:09.870
A map reference quickly followed; the longitude
and latitude of a largely empty area of the

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:10.910
Pacific Ocean.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:16.380
“Agent Paratos, your new objective is to
lure the creature into the open, by any means

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:17.380
necessary.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:19.040
GOC out.”

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.890
Slipping the communicator back into his pocket,
Paratos unclicked the elevator’s stop switch,

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:25.720
and pressed one of the numbered floor buttons.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.640
He already knew which one would take him back
down to SCP-682’s containment cell, and

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.730
what to do when he got there…

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:37.000
The commotion was unlike anything Paratos
had ever seen before; alarms were blaring

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.700
so loud that the sound was ringing in his
ears.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.610
Most of the Research staff were fleeing for
their lives, while heavily armed Mobile Task

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:49.800
Force operatives in tactical gear were moving
in formation to support the security personnel.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.020
Some had abandoned their posts out of sheer
terror, desperately looking for anywhere they

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:56.840
could hide in order to stay alive.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:02.080
Under the ear-splitting wail of alerts was
a cacophony of chaos, a mix of hailing gunfire

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.840
from the MTF’s weapons, the crumble of walls
as the facility around them was being torn

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:11.490
apart, and the angered, vicious roars of the
rampaging reptile.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:16.350
All hands were on deck, the Foundation – while
always on high alert – had been caught completely

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.790
unprepared for a containment breach.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:23.880
SCP-682 was making short work of any living
personnel in its path, carving them up with

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:29.140
its claws or devouring them while they were
still alive, laving a trail of blood and destruction

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:30.410
in its wake.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:34.850
Any survivors on site were quickly instructed
to retreat into the Command Centre, and Paratos

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.810
casually slid in alongside a gaggle of panicking
researchers.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:44.430
He recognized one from the mess hall; Researcher
Foster, wide-eyed and disassociating in shock,

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:48.030
the blood of his colleague Rees still wet
on his face and lab coat.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:51.950
The outer doors of the Command Center were
locked down, while all efforts inside were

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:56.530
put towards coordinating the re-containment
effort, tracking the escaped anomaly’s movements

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:58.770
via a live satellite feed.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.290
Agent Paratos had to think and act fast.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:05.620
The Hard to Destroy Reptile was already making
its way towards water, where GOC fighter jets

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:10.430
would enrage it in order to make the anomaly
follow them to the target area.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:16.340
But if the Foundation had a chance to recapture
SCP-682, his entire mission would be declared

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:17.690
a failure.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:21.820
Checking that nobody was watching, Paratos
snuck over to a computer terminal.

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:26.100
Looking underneath, he located a panel and
unscrewed it as quickly and inconspicuously

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.250
as he could.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:31.030
Reaching for his communicator, he pulled the
device into one of the ports underneath the

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:35.530
terminal, and it instantly went to work, installing
proprietary software that would block any

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.950
outbound Foundation communication.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:38.950
“H-hey!”

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:42.040
Foster stammered, spotting Paratos sabotaging
the terminal.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:43.040
“Hey, hey!

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.040
What’s he doing?”

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:47.540
Within moments, the researcher had gained
the attention of the surviving security personnel,

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:50.169
who rushed over and tackled the saboteur.

00:06:50.169 --> 00:06:55.130
The officers restrained Paratos, one grabbing
each of his arms as they held him in place.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:56.990
But it was too late.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:07:00.610
Communications suddenly went offline, cutting
the Command Center off from the rest of the

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:01.610
Foundation outside.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:06.000
“Who are you?” one of the security guards
yelled over and over again, punching the infiltrator

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.030
in the gut each time.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:08.630
“Who do you work for?”

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:12.270
Fighting through the pain, Paratos had kept
his jaw clenched so tightly that he could

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:15.580
feel his teeth grinding together, like they
were about to crack.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:19.560
“We still have access to all saved internal
data,” one of the Foundation technicians

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:23.690
– a man named Baxter – chimed in after
examining the computer terminal.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:27.060
“According to this security footage, he’s
the one who sprung 682.”

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:29.230
“D-do we still have no comms?”

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:32.970
Foster asked timidly, still trying to wipe
Rees’ blood off his coat.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.650
“Negative,” the technician answered.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:39.760
“Whatever that device is, it’s deactivated
every way we have to transmit… we can still

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:40.760
receive, though.”

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:45.280
His voice trailed off, reducing to match the
low silence that was now filling the Command

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.280
Centre.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:50.240
Everyone in the room, including the restrained
Agent Paratos, had gradually turned to face

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.310
the massive screens, displaying the satellite
footage of SCP-682.

00:07:54.310 --> 00:08:00.180
It was small, but visible thanks to how zoomed
in the orbital lens was.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:04.900
The Hard to Destroy Reptile had reached the
water, and was making its way across the ocean.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:10.580
At the same time, the bird’s eye feed of
the Pacific showed something else, a tiny,

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:14.880
winged object barrelling through the air at
almost supersonic speeds.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.960
“That’s a fighter jet,” one of the security
guards declared, breaking the tense silence.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.970
“Probably a Raptor by the look of it.”

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:25.440
It was, in fact, a fighter jet speeding towards
SCP-682.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:30.190
The creature seemed unaware of the aircraft,
it was merely traveling in the opposite direction,

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:36.160
but the pair were on a direct course with
each other, bound to meet at any moment now…

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:40.099
Something ejected from the F-22 the exact
moment it passed over the Hard to Destroy

00:08:40.099 --> 00:08:44.750
Reptile, followed almost instantly by a bright
flash of light.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:49.380
A huge explosion engulfed the area of the
ocean where the creature had been, a ball

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:52.451
of radioactive fire in the middle of the water.

00:08:52.451 --> 00:08:57.480
The Foundation personnel in the Command Center
watched in horror at the nuclear detonation,

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.550
holding their breath, dreading what they would
see when the mushroom cloud dissipated.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:06.120
“He’s with the Coalition,” the nearest
security guard realized, referring to Paratos.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:11.110
It was well known among staff that the SCP
Foundation maintained a somewhat fragile alliance

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:12.880
with the Global Occult Coalition.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.529
While they had historically worked together
when their goals aligned, the GOC were more

00:09:17.529 --> 00:09:22.600
intent on destroying anomalies wholesale,
wiping them from existence in order to protect

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:25.600
the United Nations and peoples of the world.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.421
The Foundation, on the other hand, although
never afraid to get their hands dirty, but

00:09:29.421 --> 00:09:34.990
more of an emphasis on containment and study
of anomalies, rather than outright extermination.

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:38.020
“What the hell have you done, you maniac?”

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:40.330
yelled one of the doctors trapped in the Command
Centre.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:44.140
“That monstrosity needed to be eliminated,”
Paratos argued back.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:48.590
“While you were wasting time keeping it
submerged in acid, ready to break out again

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:51.770
and start slaughtering people, we acted.

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:52.910
And look for yourselves, it worked.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:57.500
You should be thanking the GOC and me, because
it turns out SCP-682 wasn’t invincible after

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:58.500
all.”

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:01.560
“No, it-it isn’t,” Foster spoke up.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.190
“Rees and I were talking about it earlier,
he said SCP-682’s greatest strength isn’t

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:07.190
being invulnerable.

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:08.810
It’s being adaptable.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:14.480
It can change itself, mutate in response to
damage, or in order to survive a hostile environment.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:16.250
And you just… you haven’t killed it.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.910
You’ve allowed it to adapt to nukes.”

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:21.870
On the screens, something burst from underneath
the water.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:27.200
The overhead view from the satellite feed
filled the display an image of a dark, rocky

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:28.200
surface.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.340
“Who zoomed in on the feed?

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:30.350
Pull it back!”

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:34.030
Baxter announced, trying to hide what they
were all afraid of: that the footage it wasn’t

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:38.520
zoomed in at all; it was just looking at something
much bigger now.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:43.360
As the camera zoomed out, increasing its field
of view, what looked like a dark surface on

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.850
screen now looked to be a huge mass of scales.

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:52.220
The satellite feed pulled further back, revealing
the full extent of what was now standing in

00:10:52.220 --> 00:11:01.180
the Pacific Ocean: it was SCP-682, not only
still alive, but much, much bigger than before.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:05.260
The creature had adapted to the intense heat
of the nuclear payload that had been dropped

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:09.570
on it, soaking up the bomb’s radiation and
using it as a way to increase its body mass

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:11.930
several million times over.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:15.050
“Uhh, it looks like we have a…”

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:21.190
Baxter said dumbfounded, eyes widened in horror,
“A kaiju on our hands.”

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:25.840
Paratos stared up at the screen, looking at
the monstrous Reptile now so much bigger than

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:26.860
before.

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:31.680
Even from the satellite’s current overhead
angle, SCP-682 looked like it could now tower

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:36.070
over most buildings, save for maybe exceptionally
tall skyscrapers.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:41.200
What’s more, the creature was thrashing
and roaring in the ocean, entering an enraged

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:47.240
state now infinitely more devasting thanks
to its newfound size… and it was all Paratos’

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:48.240
fault.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:49.240
“Hey!”

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:50.410
Foster yelled at the restrained GOC operative.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:54.140
“We need to get the word out, and the rest
of this facility is still on lockdown.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:56.450
Whatever you did to disable communications,
reverse it.”

00:11:56.450 --> 00:11:57.740
“What good will that do?”

00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:59.490
Paratos asked in a blind panic.

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:01.470
“Look at the size of that abomination now.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:02.560
It’ll wipe us all out.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:04.240
Oh god, what have I done?”

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:08.740
The agent was shocked into silence by a sudden
slap; the timid Researcher Foster had struck

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:10.270
him sharply across the face.

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:13.880
“Cry about it later, this is on you, but
do you want to be part of the solution or

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:15.480
keep being part of the problem?”

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:17.680
Foster yelled, gripping Paratos by the collar.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:18.680
“I…

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:20.260
I can’t reverse it,” he replied.

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:22.640
“I blocked all outbound Foundation transmissions.

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:25.209
My comm device is the only thing that wasn’t
affected.”

00:12:25.209 --> 00:12:29.990
“Good enough,” Foster said, holding a
hand out to Baxter who passed him the confiscated

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:30.990
GOC tech.

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.400
“Then you’re going to contact the Coalition
for us.”

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:34.460
“What for?”

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:38.010
“The Foundation has a joint project we’ve
been working on with them and Hy-Brasil for

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:42.390
just this sort of occasion,” the researcher
stated, holding out the device to Agent Paratos

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:44.870
as the security finally released his arms.

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:48.029
“If there was ever a time we needed it,
it’s now.”

00:12:48.029 --> 00:12:49.149
“Wait, you mean…”

00:12:49.149 --> 00:12:51.240
Paratos finally realized what Foster meant.

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:53.880
“You’re talking about the Dragonslayer,
aren’t you?”

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:55.640
The next hour flew by.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:13:00.940
Time seemed to be moving faster, as the newly
mutated SCP-682 swam through the waters of

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:01.940
the Pacific.

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:05.640
It was traveling in a northwest direction;
the collection of Foundation personnel still

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:09.920
locked in the Command Center having projected
that it was heading for the nearest landmass,

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:13.010
which meant it would reach Hawaii any moment.

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:17.740
Throughout that entire time, Foster, Paratos,
and Baxter were sending urgent communiques

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:22.310
to the Global Occult Coalition, trying to
get them to cooperate and engage in contact

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:26.620
with the wider SCP Foundation beyond the facility
they were currently stuck in.

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:31.550
Not one of them knew which was harder, trying
to kill an unkillable lizard that had absorbed

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:37.440
a nuclear blast, or trying to force the GOC
and Foundation to work together without arguing

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:40.410
over whose fault this whole mess was.

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:46.220
All the while, the towering, monstrous reptilian
form of SCP-682 was getting closer and closer

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:47.870
to its destination…

00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:53.680
Drawing itself up to its full, enlarged height,
SCP-682 roared, large enough to block out

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:58.080
sunlight and cast an ominous shadow over the
Hawaiian coastline.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:02.630
Onlookers watched in terror as the Hard to
Destroy Reptile rose from the water, bearing

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:06.910
down on their shores with the intent to slaughter
every living thing on the island.

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:11.910
Then, just as SCP-682 neared the landmass,
the creature paused.

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:17.760
It could hear something getting closer, getting
louder as it approached from behind – no,

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:23.690
above – and with it came the sound of…
someone reciting poetry?

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:27.600
Before the creature could turn to face its
attacker, the piercing metal of the Cold Iron

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:31.130
Sword plunged through its scaly back and out
through its front.

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:35.320
The Hard to Destroy Reptile roared at the
searing pain of the stab wound, whipping its

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.960
tail as it thrashed in its fury.

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:42.170
Something heavy and metallic deflected a swipe
of the creature’s tail; it was the huge,

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:45.510
robotic arm of a gigantic mechanized titan.

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:50.170
It was SCP-5514, the Dragonslayer.

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:55.750
Controlled by its skilled pilots, with a swift,
fluid motion, SCP-5514 delivered a brutal

00:14:55.750 --> 00:15:00.170
backhanded strike to the Reptile’s snout,
staggering the beast while also withdrawing

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:03.339
its mighty Cold Iron Sword at the same time.

00:15:03.339 --> 00:15:09.280
SCP-682 growled in discontent; even its infamously
adaptable healing abilities were halted by

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:12.000
a wound from the Dragonslayer’s blade.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:15.410
Watched by the innocent civilians on the island,
and the Foundation through their satellite

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:20.820
above, the kaiju-killing mech assumed a battle-ready
stance, as the Hard to Destroy Reptile reeled

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:22.399
back to roar.

00:15:22.399 --> 00:15:27.610
The moment it opened its sharp-toothed jaws,
a fiery atomic blast came spewing forth from

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:28.610
SCP-682.

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:34.610
It had adapted not only to withstand the nuclear
weapon the GOC had used on it, but also developed

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:38.430
the ability to unleash a burst of radioactive
fire.

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:42.620
Within the Dragonslayer, the pilots braced
for impact as the blast struck the outer armor

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:43.880
of the mech.

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:49.050
Thankfully, through the conceptual engineering
used when building it, SCP-5514 was welded

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:54.089
to the Earth itself, as well as the very concept
of humanity’s resilience.

00:15:54.089 --> 00:16:00.450
That gave it just enough protection against
the onslaught of SCP-682’s newfound ability.

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:05.240
Holding up one arm to defend itself, the Dragonslayer
reached upwards for its Rounded Recoiling

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:08.950
Plasma – the disc it wore as a hat – with
its free hand.

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:14.130
With a spin, it flung the razor-edged weapon
at SCP-682 like a frisbee.

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:19.120
It collided with the Hard to Destroy Reptile,
still unleashing its nuclear fire-breath,

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:25.190
and staggered the crocodilian creature for
just a moment, causing its blast to stop.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:30.279
SCP-5514’s commanded the mech to charge
forward as the Rounded Recoiling Plasma disc

00:16:30.279 --> 00:16:34.640
spun back through the air and returned to
the Dragonslayer’s hand thanks to its built-in

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:36.260
electromagnets.

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:41.500
Within the robotic vehicle, the Thousand Word
Arrows were still loudly reciting their poetry.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:45.320
The mech’s strangest weapon was a group
of seven poets who would rhyme and chant about

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:51.370
the death and defeat of the large-scale monsters
SCP-5514 was designed to fight.

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:55.800
Their voices were being blared out of the
Dragonslayer at a loud enough volume to enrage

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:58.279
SCP-682 further.

00:16:58.279 --> 00:17:02.870
It roared again, and then started rushing
towards its adversary, the pair of colossal

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:06.559
combatants seemingly charging into a head-on
collision.

00:17:06.559 --> 00:17:10.799
As it placed one mechanical foot before the
other, huge splashes erupting as its feet

00:17:10.799 --> 00:17:15.279
impacted the surface of the ocean, the Dragonslayer
opened fire.

00:17:15.279 --> 00:17:19.970
The Beowulf-Sigured Railgun mounted on its
shoulder began launching electromagnetic energy

00:17:19.970 --> 00:17:22.329
bolts at SCP-682.

00:17:22.329 --> 00:17:26.549
The weapon used anomalously altered gravity
to blast its targets.

00:17:26.549 --> 00:17:30.520
With each hit, the energy projectiles seared
through the outer scales of the Hard to Destroy

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:34.539
Reptile, while making the towering creature
dramatically heavier.

00:17:34.539 --> 00:17:40.359
Each projectile impacted at super terminal
velocities, making SCP-682’s already enlarged

00:17:40.359 --> 00:17:43.889
body feel heavier and slower with every hit.

00:17:43.889 --> 00:17:48.909
Each step it took seemed to sink deeper under
the ocean, waves lapping at the Reptile’s

00:17:48.909 --> 00:17:52.730
damaged scales, sea salt washing over its
gaping wounds.

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:56.399
By the time it was about to clash with the
Dragonslayer, the Hard to Destroy Reptile

00:17:56.399 --> 00:18:00.909
felt such huge forces of additional weight
acting on it that its whole body was being

00:18:00.909 --> 00:18:06.869
pushed further and further below water level,
feet sinking heavily into the seafloor below.

00:18:06.869 --> 00:18:12.929
Still standing upright, SCP-5514 raised its
Cold Iron Sword and brought it down in one

00:18:12.929 --> 00:18:17.559
fell swipe, cleaving the creature’s head
from its shoulders, before wasting no time

00:18:17.559 --> 00:18:21.320
slicing up what was left of its nuclear-enlarged
body.

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:25.929
Before long, an area of the Pacific Ocean
was red with SCP-682’s blood.

00:18:25.929 --> 00:18:29.989
Victorious for now, the Dragonslayer began
gathering up the remains of the Hard to Destroy

00:18:29.989 --> 00:18:33.639
Reptile before it had a chance to reform its
body.

00:18:33.639 --> 00:18:37.779
From the Command Center, the Foundation personnel
cheered and whooped excitedly.

00:18:37.779 --> 00:18:41.259
Even Agent Paratos found himself overwhelmed
with relief.

00:18:41.259 --> 00:18:44.919
Although he’d been sent with the intention
of assisting them in killing the Hard to Destroy

00:18:44.919 --> 00:18:49.649
Reptile, the mission that the GOC had sent
him on had only made the creature more of

00:18:49.649 --> 00:18:50.649
a threat.

00:18:50.649 --> 00:18:55.009
For now, at least, it was defeated, although
Paratos’ celebration of that fact was cut

00:18:55.009 --> 00:18:58.859
short when one of the Foundation doctors tapped
him on the shoulder.

00:18:58.859 --> 00:19:01.690
“Excuse me, GOC Agent Paratos, isn’t it?”

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:02.690
the man asked.

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:05.909
“My name is Doctor Bright, and this here
is my associate Doctor Clef.

00:19:05.909 --> 00:19:10.119
We’d like to have a word with you about
the ‘incident’ you just caused…”

00:19:10.119 --> 00:19:17.100
Now go and check out “SCP-682 CRAZIEST MUTATIONS
AND ADAPTATIONS” and “SCP-682 WAYS THE

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:21.510
SCP FOUNDATION TRIED TO KILL HARD TO DESTROY
REPTILE” to see some of the extreme lengths

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:25.950
the Foundation has gone to in their attempts
to kill SCP-682!

